---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { authorSchema } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";


const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-03-15", modified: "2026-03-15" });
const title = "Ryobi ONE+ 18V Tools Review 2026";
const description = "An in-depth Ryobi tools review: the ONE+ 18V platform, build quality, performance vs competitors, and which tools are worth buying.";

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Reviews",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Ryobi Tools Review 2026",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/ryobi-tools-review/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Are Ryobi batteries interchangeable between all ONE+ tools?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes. Every ONE+ 18V battery — from the compact 1.5Ah to the large 6.0Ah HP — fits and powers every ONE+ 18V tool ever made, dating back to the platform's introduction in 1996. However, HP-designated tools will only unlock their maximum performance when paired with HP batteries. Standard batteries will work in HP tools but at reduced power output. The 40V battery platform is completely separate and is not compatible with 18V ONE+ tools.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How long do Ryobi batteries last before needing replacement?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Under typical homeowner use (weekend projects, seasonal yard work), Ryobi lithium-ion batteries last approximately 3 to 5 years or 500+ charge cycles before capacity degrades noticeably. Heavy users may see degradation sooner. Store batteries indoors at room temperature — extreme heat and cold significantly shorten lithium cell lifespan. Never leave batteries on the charger indefinitely; charge them when depleted and store at roughly 50% charge if they will not be used for extended periods.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is Ryobi made by the same company as Milwaukee?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes. Both Ryobi (power tools) and Milwaukee are owned by Techtronic Industries (TTI), a Hong Kong-based multinational. TTI also owns Hart Tools, Hoover, and Dirt Devil. However, Ryobi and Milwaukee operate as completely independent brands with separate engineering teams, manufacturing facilities, and product strategies. They share no components, batteries, or technology directly. Think of it like how Toyota and Lexus are made by the same parent company but serve completely different market segments.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can Ryobi tools handle hardwood like oak and maple?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The HP brushless tools handle hardwood reasonably well for typical homeowner tasks — drilling holes, driving screws, making crosscuts. The impact driver drives lag bolts into oak without hesitation, and the circular saw crosscuts maple 2x stock cleanly. However, sustained heavy-duty hardwood work (ripping long boards, mortising, extensive drilling of large-diameter holes) will drain batteries quickly and push the motors harder than they are designed for. For extensive hardwood projects, consider supplementing with a corded tool for the most demanding operations.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best Ryobi starter kit for a new homeowner?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The ideal starter purchase is the ONE+ HP Brushless drill/driver + impact driver combo kit (typically $149 to $179 with two HP batteries and charger). These two tools cover 80% of all homeowner tasks. From there, add a cordless inflator ($30 bare), a string trimmer ($79 to $99 bare), and a multi-tool ($60 to $70 bare) as needs arise. This core set of 5 tools plus 2 HP batteries will handle virtually any project a homeowner encounters for under $400 total.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Are Ryobi tools good enough for professional side work?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For occasional side work — helping a friend remodel a bathroom, building a deck on weekends, doing handyman projects — Ryobi HP tools are entirely adequate. The tools deliver sufficient power and precision for quality results. However, if side work evolves into a regular income source with daily tool use, plan to transition to a professional-grade brand within 1 to 2 years. The economics shift at the professional duty cycle: paying more upfront for tools that last 5 to 7 years under daily use is cheaper than replacing consumer tools every 1 to 2 years.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does Ryobi ever go on sale?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Absolutely. Home Depot runs aggressive Ryobi promotions during Memorial Day, Fourth of July, Labor Day, Black Friday, and holiday season. The best deals are typically \"buy a battery kit, get a free bare tool\" promotions and multi-tool combo kits. Black Friday 2025 saw 6-tool combo kits with two batteries and a charger for under $200 — extraordinary value. If your purchase is not urgent, waiting for a seasonal sale can save 25% to 40% compared to regular pricing.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "BlogPosting",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: {
          "@type": "Person",
          name: "Alex Rivers",
      },
      datePublished: dates.published,
      publisher: {
          "@type": "Organization",
          name: "The Honest Reviewers",
      }
    },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
    title={title}
    description={description}
    ogType="article"
    article={{
      publishedTime: dates.published,
      modifiedTime: dates.modified,
      author: "Alex Rivers",
      section: "Tools",
      tags: [
        "ryobi tools review",
        "tools",
      ],
    }}
    jsonLd={schema}
    stickyCta={{
        text: "Jump to Verdict",
        link: "#verdict",
    }}
>
    <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
        <!-- Hero Section -->
        <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
            <div
                class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10"
                style="background-image: url('data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg width=\'60\' height=\'60\' viewBox=\'0 0 60 60\' xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'none\' fill-rule=\'evenodd\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'%23ffffff\' fill-opacity=\'1\'%3E%3Cpath d=\'M36 34v-4h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2h-4zm0-30V0h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2V6h4V4h-4zM6 34v-4H4v4H0v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2H6zM6 4V0H4v4H0v2h4v4h2V6h4V4H6z\'/%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/svg%3E');"
            >
            </div>
            <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
                <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
                    <TrustBadge />
                </div>
                <h1
                    class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8"
                >
                    <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Ryobi Tools</span> Review 2026: Honest Assessment of the ONE+ 18V System
                </h1>
                <p
                    class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto"
                >
                    Over 300 tools on a single battery platform. Unbeatable prices. But is Ryobi actually good — or just cheap? We break down what works, what doesn't, and who this brand is really built for.
                </p>
                <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
                    <a
                        href="#verdict"
                        class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
                    >
                        Skip to The Verdict
                    </a>
                    <a
                        href="#platform"
                        class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
                    >
                        Read Full Review
                    </a>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Author & Meta -->
        <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
              <div>
                <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
                <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
            <div class="hidden sm:block">
              <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
              <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
            </div>
            <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
              <SocialProof users="7.8k" />
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Table of Contents -->
        <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
            <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
              <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#1E90FF" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="8" y1="6" x2="21" y2="6"></line><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="21" y2="12"></line><line x1="8" y1="18" x2="21" y2="18"></line><line x1="3" y1="6" x2="3.01" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="12" x2="3.01" y2="12"></line><line x1="3" y1="18" x2="3.01" y2="18"></line></svg>
              In This Guide
            </h2>
            <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
              <a href="#platform" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. The ONE+ Platform: Why the Battery System Matters More Than Any Individual Tool</a>
              <a href="#battery-technology-hp-vs-standard" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Battery Technology: HP vs Standard vs High Capacity</a>
              <a href="#build-quality-the-honest-truth" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Build Quality: The Honest Truth</a>
              <a href="#ryobi-tools-that-are-genuinely" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Ryobi Tools That Are Genuinely Excellent</a>
              <a href="#ryobi-tools-you-should-skip" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Ryobi Tools You Should Skip</a>
              <a href="#ryobi-vs-the-competition-honest" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Ryobi vs the Competition: Honest Tier Rankings</a>
              <a href="#the-home-depot-exclusivity-factor" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. The Home Depot Exclusivity Factor</a>
              <a href="#warranty-and-durability-what-to" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Warranty and Durability: What to Expect</a>
              <a href="#the-40v-platform-when-18v" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. The 40V Platform: When 18V Is Not Enough</a>
              <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. FAQ</a>
            </nav>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Content -->
        <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-16 lg:py-24">
            <div
                class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed"
            >
                <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic border-l-4 border-[#38BDF8] pl-6 bg-gray-50 py-4 rounded-r-xl">
                    Ryobi occupies a unique position in the power tool market. It is not the cheapest. It is not the most powerful. But with the largest cordless tool ecosystem in existence and a price-to-performance ratio that no competitor can match, it has quietly become the best-selling power tool brand in North America — and it is not hard to understand why.
                </p>

                <p>
                    If you spend any time in online tool forums, you will encounter an almost religious fervor around power tool brands. DeWalt loyalists dismiss anything yellow-green. Milwaukee devotees scoff at anything without the FUEL badge. Makita fans insist that teal is the only serious color. And in the middle of this brand tribalism sits Ryobi — simultaneously the most popular and most dismissed power tool brand on the planet.
                </p>

                <p>
                    The dismissal is unfair. The popularity is deserved. But neither tells the complete story. This review is not a cheerleading exercise or a hit piece. It is a thorough, honest assessment of the Ryobi ONE+ 18V system in 2026 — what it does exceptionally well, where it falls short, which specific tools are genuinely excellent, and which ones you should avoid. If you are considering investing in the Ryobi ecosystem, this guide gives you the information you actually need.
                </p>

                <h2 id="platform" class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
                    The ONE+ Platform: Why the Battery System Matters More Than Any Individual Tool
                </h2>
                <p>
                    The single most important thing to understand about Ryobi is that you are not buying tools — you are buying into a <strong>platform</strong>. The ONE+ 18V battery system is the foundation that everything else is built on, and its scale is genuinely unprecedented in the industry.
                </p>

                <p>
                    As of early 2026, the Ryobi ONE+ platform encompasses over <strong>300 cordless tools</strong> that all share the same 18V lithium-ion battery pack. That number is not a typo. Three hundred. No other manufacturer comes close — DeWalt's 20V MAX system covers roughly 250 tools, Milwaukee M18 covers about 200, and Makita's 18V LXT platform reaches approximately 275. But Ryobi's ecosystem extends far beyond traditional power tools into categories that no other brand touches: cordless glue guns, soldering irons, portable fans, Bluetooth speakers, bug zappers, inflators, heated blankets, and even a cordless coffee maker. Some of these are gimmicky. Some are genuinely useful. But they all share the same battery.
                </p>

                <p>
                    This platform approach creates a powerful economic flywheel. Once you own three or four Ryobi batteries, every additional tool purchase is cheaper because you can buy "bare tool" versions without batteries. A Ryobi circular saw that costs $129 as a kit with battery and charger costs only $79 as a bare tool if you already own batteries. Across a workshop full of 10 to 15 tools, the cumulative savings from buying bare tools is often $300 to $500 compared to buying each tool individually with its own battery.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Crucially, Ryobi has maintained backward compatibility across the entire ONE+ battery line since its introduction in 1996. Batteries purchased today will power tools from 10 years ago, and vice versa. This 30-year backward compatibility commitment is unmatched in the industry and means your investment in Ryobi batteries is genuinely future-proof in ways that other platforms cannot guarantee.
                </p>

                <h2 id="battery-technology-hp-vs-standard" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
                    Battery Technology: HP vs Standard vs High Capacity
                </h2>
                <p>
                    Ryobi's current battery lineup includes three tiers that serve different needs. Understanding these tiers is important because battery choice directly affects tool performance.
                </p>

                <p>
                    <strong>Standard ONE+ batteries</strong> (1.5Ah and 2.0Ah) are the compact, lightweight packs included with most tool kits. They are adequate for light-duty work — driving screws, cutting trim, drilling holes in softwood — but they run out of capacity quickly under sustained heavy use and lack the high-discharge capability needed to maximize motor performance in demanding tools.
                </p>

                <p>
                    <strong>ONE+ HP batteries</strong> (3.0Ah, 4.0Ah, and 6.0Ah) are the game-changer that transformed Ryobi's performance reputation. HP batteries use higher-quality lithium cells arranged in a configuration that delivers significantly more current to the motor. When paired with HP-designated tools (identifiable by the "HP" badge on the tool body), the combination unlocks a brushless motor mode that delivers noticeably more power, speed, and runtime. The difference is not subtle — an HP drill with an HP battery feels like a completely different tool compared to the same drill with a standard battery. If you are buying into the Ryobi ecosystem in 2026, start with HP batteries. The standard packs are increasingly a relic.
                </p>

                <p>
                    <strong>ONE+ 4.0Ah and 6.0Ah High Capacity</strong> packs are the marathon runners — designed for runtime-hungry tools like lawn mowers, leaf blowers, and reciprocating saws that drain batteries quickly. The 6.0Ah pack is noticeably heavier, so it is not ideal for overhead work or compact tools, but for ground-level equipment like mowers and string trimmers, the extended runtime is well worth the added weight.
                </p>

                <h2 id="build-quality-the-honest-truth" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
                    Build Quality: The Honest Truth
                </h2>
                <p>
                    This is where the honest assessment gets uncomfortable for both Ryobi fans and Ryobi critics, because the truth is nuanced. Ryobi's build quality is not uniform across the product line — it ranges from impressively solid to disappointingly flimsy depending on the specific tool category.
                </p>

                <p>
                    The <strong>housing construction</strong> on most Ryobi tools is injection-molded plastic — standard for consumer-grade power tools but notably thinner and lighter than the reinforced nylon and composite housings used by DeWalt, Milwaukee, and Makita on their professional lines. This is not a functional problem for homeowner use, but it means Ryobi tools will show wear, crack, or break faster if dropped repeatedly on concrete or subjected to daily jobsite abuse. The tools are designed for weekend warrior use, not professional daily hammering.
                </p>

                <p>
                    The <strong>motor quality</strong> has improved dramatically in recent years with the expansion of brushless motors across the lineup. Ryobi's brushless tools (particularly the HP line) use genuine high-quality brushless motors that deliver performance surprisingly close to competitor brands at a fraction of the price. The older brushed motor tools, which are still sold alongside the brushless versions at lower price points, are noticeably weaker and less efficient. For any tool where performance matters, always choose the brushless/HP version even if it costs $20 to $30 more.
                </p>

                <p>
                    The <strong>chuck quality</strong> on Ryobi drills is a common and legitimate complaint. The single-sleeve keyless chucks used on most Ryobi drill/drivers have a tendency to loosen under heavy vibration, allowing bits to slip during aggressive drilling. This is not a problem for driving screws or drilling small holes in wood, but it becomes frustrating when boring large holes in hardwood or metal. The fix is to tighten the chuck more aggressively than you think is necessary, or upgrade to one of the HP models that use improved chuck mechanisms.
                </p>

                <h2 id="ryobi-tools-that-are-genuinely" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
                    Ryobi Tools That Are Genuinely Excellent
                </h2>

                <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">1. ONE+ HP Brushless Impact Driver (PBLID02)</h3>
                <p>
                    This is the single best tool in the entire Ryobi lineup and one of the best values in all of cordless tools. It delivers 1,800 in-lbs of torque — which is within 10% of the DeWalt and Milwaukee equivalents that cost twice as much. The brushless motor is efficient, compact, and powerful enough for virtually any fastening task a homeowner or even a light-duty professional will encounter. Driving 3-inch deck screws, assembling furniture, installing lag bolts, removing automotive lug nuts — this impact driver handles all of it without breaking a sweat. At approximately $99 as a bare tool, it is an absurd bargain.
                </p>

                <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">2. ONE+ HP 18V Brushless Circular Saw (PBLCS300)</h3>
                <p>
                    Ryobi's latest brushless 7-1/4 inch circular saw is a genuine surprise. It cuts through 2x lumber cleanly and quickly, features a magnesium shoe for reduced weight and improved rigidity, and includes electronic brake and LED cut line indicator. Is it as smooth as a Makita or as powerful as a DeWalt Flexvolt? No. But for framing a deck, cutting plywood for shelving, or building a shed, it performs at about 85% of the professional tools at about 40% of the price. For anyone not cutting lumber professionally eight hours a day, that is an outstanding tradeoff.
                </p>

                <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">3. ONE+ 18V String Trimmer (P20120)</h3>
                <p>
                    Ryobi's string trimmers are probably the single product category most responsible for the brand's massive market share. They are priced aggressively ($79 to $129 with battery), perform reliably for typical suburban lawn edging, start instantly (no pull cords, no mixing gas), and run quietly enough to use early on weekend mornings without angering neighbors. The auto-feed line advancement works consistently, and a 4.0Ah battery provides enough runtime to trim a standard suburban lot on a single charge. For homeowner lawn care, Ryobi trimmers are essentially the default recommendation.
                </p>

                <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">4. ONE+ 18V Multi-Tool (PCL430)</h3>
                <p>
                    Oscillating multi-tools are one of those categories where brand differences matter less than blade quality. Ryobi's multi-tool oscillates at competitive speeds, accepts standard Starlock blades from any manufacturer, and gets the job done for flush cutting, sanding, scraping, and grout removal. It is not as refined as the Bosch StarlockMax or Milwaukee M18 FUEL equivalent, but at roughly half the price, it handles 95% of multi-tool tasks identically. Pair it with quality aftermarket blades from Bosch or Diablo and you have a seriously capable tool for $70.
                </p>

                <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">5. ONE+ 18V Inflator (P737D)</h3>
                <p>
                    This is a sleeper hit that many Ryobi owners cite as their most-used tool. The cordless inflator fills car tires, bicycle tires, sports balls, pool floats, and air mattresses with preset digital pressure settings. Set your target PSI, connect the hose, press the button, and walk away — it auto-stops at exactly the right pressure. At $30 as a bare tool, it is one of those purchases that makes you wonder how you ever lived without it. Keeping one in the trunk of your car with a charged battery is genuinely life-changing for roadside tire maintenance.
                </p>

                <h2 id="ryobi-tools-you-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
                    Ryobi Tools You Should Skip
                </h2>

                <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">1. ONE+ 18V Brushed Jigsaw (P5231)</h3>
                <p>
                    The brushed jigsaw suffers from excessive vibration, imprecise blade tracking, and an underpowered motor that bogs down in anything thicker than 3/4-inch hardwood. Straight cuts wander, curved cuts are rough, and the tool feels unrefined compared to even budget corded jigsaws. If you need a cordless jigsaw, wait for the HP brushless version or buy a quality corded jigsaw for $60 that will outperform it dramatically.
                </p>

                <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">2. ONE+ 18V Miter Saw (PBT01B)</h3>
                <p>
                    Cordless miter saws are an inherently challenging proposition at 18V — they require enormous power to spin a 10-inch blade through dimensional lumber, and 18V simply does not deliver enough sustained current for heavy crosscutting. The Ryobi cordless miter saw can handle trim and light crosscuts in pine, but anything denser or thicker results in slow, labored cuts and rapid battery drain. A 4.0Ah battery provides only about 100 to 150 cuts in 2x4 pine. For a miter saw, go corded unless portability is an absolute requirement, in which case the DeWalt 20V MAX 7-1/4 inch sliding miter saw is a far better cordless option.
                </p>

                <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">3. ONE+ 18V Router (P601)</h3>
                <p>
                    The brushed Ryobi router lacks the power and speed stability needed for clean routing. It bogs down on deep passes, the collet design makes bit changes frustrating, and the depth adjustment mechanism feels imprecise. Routing is a task where motor power and speed consistency are paramount, and the 18V platform simply does not deliver enough energy for a tool that needs to spin a bit at 25,000+ RPM under load. Buy a dedicated corded router — a quality quarter-inch trim router costs $80 to $100 and will outperform any cordless 18V router on the market.
                </p>

                <h2 id="ryobi-vs-the-competition-honest" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
                    Ryobi vs the Competition: Honest Tier Rankings
                </h2>
                <p>
                    The power tool market divides roughly into three performance tiers, and understanding where Ryobi sits prevents unrealistic expectations.
                </p>

                <p>
                    <strong>Professional Tier (Tier 1):</strong> Milwaukee FUEL, DeWalt XR/Flexvolt, Makita LXT/XGT, Hilti. These are built for tradespeople who use tools 8+ hours daily on construction sites. They feature maximum power, the most durable construction, and the longest warranty coverage. They are also the most expensive, with individual tools commonly priced $199 to $399.
                </p>

                <p>
                    <strong>Prosumer/Advanced DIY Tier (Tier 2):</strong> Ryobi HP Brushless, DeWalt Atomic, Makita CXT, Bosch 18V. This is where Ryobi's HP brushless tools compete — and they compete surprisingly well. In controlled testing, Ryobi HP tools typically deliver 75% to 90% of the power output of Tier 1 tools at 40% to 60% of the price. For weekend warriors, serious DIYers, and light-duty professionals, this performance level is more than sufficient.
                </p>

                <p>
                    <strong>Entry/Budget Tier (Tier 3):</strong> Ryobi brushed tools, Black+Decker, Craftsman (non-V20 Brushless), Hart. The older brushed Ryobi tools fall into this category alongside the true budget brands. They work for basic tasks but lack the power, refinement, and durability of even Ryobi's own HP line. The price difference between brushed Ryobi and brushless HP Ryobi is typically only $20 to $40, making the brushed versions poor value in 2026.
                </p>

                <p>
                    The key insight is that <strong>Ryobi HP brushless tools punch above their price class</strong>. They deliver Tier 2 performance at a price point that is barely above Tier 3. This is the core of Ryobi's value proposition, and it is genuine. The older brushed tools, however, deliver exactly what you pay for — adequate but unimpressive performance for light-duty use.
                </p>

                <h2 id="the-home-depot-exclusivity-factor" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
                    The Home Depot Exclusivity Factor
                </h2>
                <p>
                    Ryobi is sold exclusively through Home Depot in North America. This exclusivity is both a strength and a limitation. On the positive side, Home Depot's massive retail footprint means Ryobi tools, batteries, and accessories are available within a short drive for the vast majority of Americans. Returns, warranty claims, and exchanges are handled in-store with minimal friction. Holiday sales and promotional bundles at Home Depot often offer extraordinary value — Black Friday combo kits with multiple tools, batteries, and a charger for under $200 are common.
                </p>

                <p>
                    The limitation is competition — or rather, the lack of it. Because Ryobi does not sell through Amazon (only through Home Depot's Amazon storefront at the same price), independent hardware stores, or any other retailer, there is no competitive pricing pressure between retailers. The price you see at Home Depot is the price, period. DeWalt and Milwaukee, by contrast, are sold through multiple retailers, creating price competition that sometimes results in better deals. Ryobi compensates by keeping its base prices aggressively low, but you will not find surprise bargains at a competing store.
                </p>

                <h2 id="warranty-and-durability-what-to" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
                    Warranty and Durability: What to Expect
                </h2>
                <p>
                    Ryobi offers a <strong>3-year limited warranty</strong> on all power tools and a <strong>3-year limited warranty</strong> on batteries. This is standard for consumer-grade tools but falls short of the professional brands: DeWalt offers 3 years free service plus 1 year free repair, Milwaukee provides 5-year tool warranties, and Makita offers 3-year warranties backed by their own service centers.
                </p>

                <p>
                    In practical terms, Ryobi's warranty service is handled through Home Depot's returns desk for the first 90 days and through Ryobi's own service center (operated by parent company TTI) afterward. The service center experience is generally acceptable but not exceptional — turnaround times of 2 to 4 weeks are typical for warranty repairs. Some users report difficulty getting warranty claims approved for tools that show signs of heavy use, although this is common across all brands.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Durability expectations should be calibrated realistically. A Ryobi tool used for typical homeowner tasks — weekend projects, seasonal yard work, occasional repairs — will last for many years without issues. The same tool subjected to daily professional use will likely fail within 1 to 2 years. This is not a defect; it is a design point. Ryobi tools are engineered and priced for consumer duty cycles, and judging them by professional standards is like criticizing a Honda Civic for not performing like a Ford F-250.
                </p>

                <h2 id="the-40v-platform-when-18v" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
                    The 40V Platform: When 18V Is Not Enough
                </h2>
                <p>
                    Ryobi also offers a separate <strong>40V battery platform</strong> specifically designed for outdoor power equipment — lawn mowers, chainsaws, snow blowers, leaf blowers, and hedge trimmers. The 40V system uses larger, higher-capacity batteries that deliver the sustained power these demanding tools require. The 40V batteries are <em>not</em> cross-compatible with ONE+ 18V tools — they are a completely separate ecosystem.
                </p>

                <p>
                    The 40V lawn mower deserves specific mention because it is one of Ryobi's most commercially successful products. The self-propelled models can mow a standard 1/4-acre suburban lawn on a single battery charge, start with a button press, run significantly quieter than gas mowers, and require virtually zero engine maintenance. For suburban homeowners tired of gas, oil changes, spark plugs, and pull cords, the Ryobi 40V mower is a revelation. Multiple independent reviews consistently rate it as the best value in cordless mowing.
                </p>

                <p>
                    However, the 40V chainsaw and snow blower are less impressive. Both are adequate for light-duty use — trimming small branches and clearing 4 to 6 inches of snow from a short driveway — but they lack the raw power for heavy cutting or deep snowfall. If you regularly fell trees larger than 10 inches in diameter or live in a heavy snow region, gas-powered equipment or a higher-voltage platform like EGO 56V is a better investment.
                </p>

