                <h2 id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
                    Frequently Asked Questions
                </h2>

                <div class="space-y-6">
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Are Ryobi batteries interchangeable between all ONE+ tools?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">Yes. Every ONE+ 18V battery — from the compact 1.5Ah to the large 6.0Ah HP — fits and powers every ONE+ 18V tool ever made, dating back to the platform's introduction in 1996. However, HP-designated tools will only unlock their maximum performance when paired with HP batteries. Standard batteries will work in HP tools but at reduced power output. The 40V battery platform is completely separate and is not compatible with 18V ONE+ tools.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How long do Ryobi batteries last before needing replacement?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">Under typical homeowner use (weekend projects, seasonal yard work), Ryobi lithium-ion batteries last approximately 3 to 5 years or 500+ charge cycles before capacity degrades noticeably. Heavy users may see degradation sooner. Store batteries indoors at room temperature — extreme heat and cold significantly shorten lithium cell lifespan. Never leave batteries on the charger indefinitely; charge them when depleted and store at roughly 50% charge if they will not be used for extended periods.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Ryobi made by the same company as Milwaukee?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">Yes. Both Ryobi (power tools) and Milwaukee are owned by Techtronic Industries (TTI), a Hong Kong-based multinational. TTI also owns Hart Tools, Hoover, and Dirt Devil. However, Ryobi and Milwaukee operate as completely independent brands with separate engineering teams, manufacturing facilities, and product strategies. They share no components, batteries, or technology directly. Think of it like how Toyota and Lexus are made by the same parent company but serve completely different market segments.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can Ryobi tools handle hardwood like oak and maple?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">The HP brushless tools handle hardwood reasonably well for typical homeowner tasks — drilling holes, driving screws, making crosscuts. The impact driver drives lag bolts into oak without hesitation, and the circular saw crosscuts maple 2x stock cleanly. However, sustained heavy-duty hardwood work (ripping long boards, mortising, extensive drilling of large-diameter holes) will drain batteries quickly and push the motors harder than they are designed for. For extensive hardwood projects, consider supplementing with a corded tool for the most demanding operations.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best Ryobi starter kit for a new homeowner?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">The ideal starter purchase is the <strong>ONE+ HP Brushless drill/driver + impact driver combo kit</strong> (typically $149 to $179 with two HP batteries and charger). These two tools cover 80% of all homeowner tasks. From there, add a <strong>cordless inflator</strong> ($30 bare), a <strong>string trimmer</strong> ($79 to $99 bare), and a <strong>multi-tool</strong> ($60 to $70 bare) as needs arise. This core set of 5 tools plus 2 HP batteries will handle virtually any project a homeowner encounters for under $400 total.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Are Ryobi tools good enough for professional side work?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">For occasional side work — helping a friend remodel a bathroom, building a deck on weekends, doing handyman projects — Ryobi HP tools are entirely adequate. The tools deliver sufficient power and precision for quality results. However, if side work evolves into a regular income source with daily tool use, plan to transition to a professional-grade brand within 1 to 2 years. The economics shift at the professional duty cycle: paying more upfront for tools that last 5 to 7 years under daily use is cheaper than replacing consumer tools every 1 to 2 years.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Does Ryobi ever go on sale?</h3>
                        <p class="text-gray-700">Absolutely. Home Depot runs aggressive Ryobi promotions during Memorial Day, Fourth of July, Labor Day, Black Friday, and holiday season. The best deals are typically "buy a battery kit, get a free bare tool" promotions and multi-tool combo kits. Black Friday 2025 saw 6-tool combo kits with two batteries and a charger for under $200 — extraordinary value. If your purchase is not urgent, waiting for a seasonal sale can save 25% to 40% compared to regular pricing.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>

